WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.640
I think this might be it.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:12.231
This paper is called Monte Carlo Geometry
Processing, and in my opinion, this may one

00:00:12.231 --> 00:00:16.650
of the best, if not the best computer graphics
paper of the year.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:22.029
There will be so many amazing results, I first
thought this one paper could be the topic

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:27.279
of ten Two Minute Papers videos, but I will
attempt to cram everything into this one video.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:32.360
It is quite challenging to explain, so please
bear with me, I’ll try my best.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:38.210
To even have a fighting chance at understanding
what is going on here, first, we start out

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:43.900
one of the most beautiful topics in computer
graphics, which is none other than light transport.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.770
To create a beautiful light simulation, we
have to solve something that we call the rendering

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:49.770
equation.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:55.030
For practical cases, it is currently impossible
to solve it like we usually solve any other

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:56.080
equation.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.110
However, what we can do, is that we can choose
a random ray, simulate its path as it bounces

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:07.880
around in the scene and compute how it interacts
with the objects and material within this

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:08.880
scene.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.710
As we do it with more and more rays, we get
more information about what we see in the

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:17.610
scene, that’s good, but, look, it is noisy.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:23.870
As we add even more rays, this noise slowly
evens out, and in the end, we get a perfectly

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:24.870
clean image.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:30.080
This takes place with the help of a technique
that we call Monte Carlo integration, which

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.830
involves randomness.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:36.970
At the risk of oversimplifying the situation,
in essence, this technique says that we cannot

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:42.700
solve the problem, but we can take samples
from the problem, and if we do it in a smart

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.990
way, eventually, we will be able to solve
it.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:51.541
In light transport, the problem is the rendering
equation, which we cannot solve, but we can

00:01:51.541 --> 00:01:55.670
take samples, one sample is simulating one
ray.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.920
If we have enough rays, we have a solution.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.920
However, it hasn’t always been like this.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.770
Before Monte Carlo integration, light transport
was done through a technique called Radiosity.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:12.709
The key issue with Radiosity was that the
geometry of the scene had to be sliced up

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:18.510
into many small pieces, and the light scattering
events had to be evaluated between these small

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:19.540
pieces.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:24.409
It could not handle all light transport phenomena
and the geometry processing part was a major

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:30.389
headache, and Monte Carlo integration was
a revelation that breathed new life into this

00:02:30.389 --> 00:02:31.389
field.

00:02:31.389 --> 00:02:36.930
However, there are still many geometry processing
problems that include these headaches, and,

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:42.279
hold on to your papers, because this paper
shows us that we can apply Monte Carlo integration

00:02:42.279 --> 00:02:44.840
to many of these problems too.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:51.180
For instance, one, it can resolve the rich
external and internal structure of this ant.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:56.239
With traditional techniques, this would normally
take more than 14 hours and 30 gigabytes of

00:02:56.239 --> 00:03:02.270
memory, but, if we apply Monte Carlo integration
to this problem, we can get a somewhat noisy

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.889
preview of the result in less than one minute.

00:03:05.889 --> 00:03:12.669
Of course, over time, as we compute more samples,
the noise clears up and we get this beautiful

00:03:12.669 --> 00:03:14.189
final result.

00:03:14.189 --> 00:03:18.700
And the concept can be used for so much more,
it truly makes my head spin.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.459
Let’s discuss six amazing applications,
while noting that there are so many more in

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:27.760
the paper, which you can and should check
out in the video description.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:34.249
For instance, two, it can also compute a CT
scan of the infamous shovelnose frog that

00:03:34.249 --> 00:03:39.919
you see here, and instead of creating the
full 3D solution, we only have to compute

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:43.799
a 2D slice of it, which is much, much cheaper.

00:03:43.799 --> 00:03:50.109
Three, it can also edit these curves, and
note that the key part is that we can do that

00:03:50.109 --> 00:03:55.959
without the major headache of creating an
intermediate triangle mesh geometry for it.

00:03:55.959 --> 00:04:01.180
Four, it also supports denoising techniques,
so we don’t have to compute too many samples

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.389
to get a clear image or piece of geometry.

00:04:04.389 --> 00:04:10.450
Five, performing a Helmholtz-Hodge decomposition
with this method is also possible.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:15.290
This is a technique that is used widely in
many domains, for instance, it is responsible

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:21.739
to ensure the stability of many fluid simulation
programs, and this technique can also produce

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:23.550
these decompositions.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:29.699
And interestingly, here it is used to represent
3D objects without the usual triangle meshes

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:31.990
that we use in computer graphics.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:36.260
Six, it supports multiple importance sampling
as well.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:41.150
This means that if we have multiple sampling
strategies, multiple ways to solve a problem

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:46.259
that have different advantages and disadvantages,
it combines them in a way that we get the

00:04:46.259 --> 00:04:48.090
best of all of them.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:53.770
We had a mega-episode on multiple importance
sampling, it has lots of amazing uses in light

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:58.031
transport simulations, so if you would like
to hear more, make sure to check that video

00:04:58.031 --> 00:04:59.380
out in the video description.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.080
But wait, these are all difficult problems.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:09.150
One surely needs a PhD and years of experience
in computer graphics to implement this, right?

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:15.039
When seeing a work like this, we often ask,
okay, it does something great, but how complex

00:05:15.039 --> 00:05:16.039
is it?

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:19.120
How many days do I have to work to reimplement
it?

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.560
Please take a guess and let me know what the
guess was in the comments section.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:30.110
And now, what you see here is none other than
the source code for the core of the method,

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:34.080
and what’s even more, a bunch of implementations
of it already exist.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.790
And, if you see that a paper has been reimplemented
around day 1, you know it’s good.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:44.780
So, no wonder, this paper has been accepted
to SIGGRAPH, perhaps the most prestigious

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.610
computer graphics conference.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:51.750
It is immensely difficult to get a paper accepted
there, and I would say this one more than

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:52.750
earned it.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:57.199
Huge congratulations to the first author of
the paper, Rohan Sawhney, he is currently

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:02.330
a PhD student, and note that this was his
second published paper.

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:03.330
Unreal.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:07.110
Such a great leap for the field in just one
paper.

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:12.389
Also congratulations to professor Keenan Crane
who advised this project and many other wonderful

00:06:12.389 --> 00:06:14.310
works in the last few years.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:19.129
I cannot wait to see what they will be up
to next and I hope that now, you are just

00:06:19.129 --> 00:07:21.220
as excited.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:29.599
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

